{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794367877,"nonce":"085Zq49HhsPRLBBBnT4dgMpDcN8=","message":"**Cause found and fixed: the swapchain image was acquired before the draw decision and never handed back for a skipped frame. This corrects my earlier comments here.**\n\nEarlier I wrote that the compositor goes quiet and that the blocking acquire alone is the problem. Both were wrong. A reference app (plain winit 0.30 + wgpu 26, same surface configuration, draws only on key presses) in the same virtual KWin shows no stall in any variant (fifo-relaxed, mailbox, immediate, Poll, with and without `pre_present_notify`, software Vulkan with wl_shm, IME on): 0 of 640 keys each. So the compositor and the winit/wgpu path are fine.\n\nIn Blinc, `get_current_texture()` ran before the rebuild/animation phases decided whether to draw. A skipped frame (nothing changed, cap not elapsed) returned without presenting, and wgpu's Vulkan backend does not return such an image, so it stays held. After a few skipped frames all images are held, the next acquire blocks for the 1 s timeout, the surface is reconfigured (a new swapchain every ~1.05 s in the Wayland trace), and the loop reads no input meanwhile, which is the key burst. An instrumented build counted 78 acquire timeouts in 9 runs; presenting the image on the skip path made them 0.\n\nFix: acquire after the draw decision and present every acquired image: https://github.com/project-blinc/Blinc/pull/76 (a failed acquire reconfigures and asks for a new frame; tested with a fake swapchain).\n\n20 runs each, virtual KWin, 43 keys 120 ms apart:\n\n| build | runs with a stall (gap over 500 ms or bundled keys) | acquires over 50 ms |\n|---|---|---|\n| before, text form | 19/20 | 78 timeouts in 9 runs |\n| before, maps window | 14/20 | |\n| after, text form | 0/20 | 0 |\n| after, maps window | 0/21 | 0 |\n\nIdle cost: with the keep-alive on (current default) an idle window now presents at 60 Hz (2.9 % of a core, 60 commits per second; before 0.65 % and a median of 7.7, low only because of the stalls). With `BLINC_KEEP_ALIVE=0` it is 0.25 % and 2.5 commits per second, and no stall showed up in a 40 s static scene followed by typing. Whether the keep-alive should default to off is a separate change; I would want a measurement on a real GPU session (Mutter/KWin on DRM, occluded windows) first, and it is not in the PR.","files":null}]}